.TH YAPACC 1 "2026-11-19" "yapacc 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yapacc \- an optional companion to the package manager you already have
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yapacc
.RI [ GLOBAL\-OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.br
.B yapacc help
.I COMMAND
.br
.B yapacc docs
.RI [ TOPIC ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yapacc
works beside
.BR pacman ,
.BR apt ,
.BR dnf ,
.BR zypper ,
.BR apk ,
.B xbps
and FreeBSD's
.BR pkg .
You keep using them.
It reads what they already record, and answers four questions: why a
package is installed, what changed, what the next upgrade will do, and
whether the last change can be taken back.
When you ask it to change something, it runs the command the package
manager would run itself, after showing it to you.
.PP
It is not a package manager: it has no repository and no package format.
It keeps no copy of what the package manager knows, so a change you make
directly is never a surprise to it.
Nothing runs in the background: no daemon, timer, hook or shell alias is
installed unless you ask for one.
.PP
Read commands change nothing and work on a machine that has never used
.B yapacc
before.
Every command that changes the system shows its plan and asks first; see
.BR "THE GATE" .
.PP
Three programs are installed.
.B yapacc
is the command line and the only one that is needed.
.B yapacc\-tui
is the interactive interface started by
.BR "yapacc i" .
.B yapacc\-mcp
is the server for AI assistants started by
.BR "yapacc mcp" .
A command that needs a program or tool the machine lacks names it and
exits with status 6.
.PP
The same documentation is built into the program:
.B yapacc docs
lists the topics and
.BI "yapacc docs " TOPIC
prints one.
The topics are
getting\-started, daily, commands, workflow, why, undo, snapshots,
platform, safety, exit\-codes, json, config, get, inspection, search,
ledger, foreign, repo\-trust, aur, autorepair, plans, daemon, plugins, tui
and ai.
.SH OPTIONS
Global options go before the command:
.BR "yapacc \-\-json why bash" .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of text.
A command that has no JSON form prints nothing and exits with status 2;
the commands that have one are listed under
.BR COMMANDS .
.TP
.B \-\-script
Never prompt.
A command that would ask is refused (status 3) unless
.B \-\-yes
or
.B \-\-dryrun
is given, and the privilege tool is called so that it fails instead of
asking for a password.
.TP
.B \-\-noconfirm
Consent in advance to the question of the gate, like
.B \-\-yes
on a gated command.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read this configuration file instead of
.IR ~/.config/yapacc/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help, for the program or for one command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
Every gated command takes these two after the command.
They cannot be given together.
.TP
.B \-\-dryrun
Show the plan and the package manager's simulation; change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Apply without the question.
The plan is still shown.
.SH COMMANDS
.B yapacc \-\-help
lists the everyday commands.
.BI "yapacc help " COMMAND
prints the options of any command.
Commands marked (gated) go through the gate.
.SS Reports (read\-only)
.TP
.BI why " NAME"
Why a package is installed: the reason, the chain back to what you asked
for, what needs it, where it came from, how it was verified.
Status 4 when it is not installed.
.TP
.B timeline
Installs, upgrades and removals from the package manager's own log, newest
first.
.BI \-\-since " YYYY\-MM\-DD"
starts at a day,
.B \-\-since\-boot
at the boot before the current one,
.BI \-\-limit " N"
shows at most N changes (50 by default),
.B \-\-html
writes one self\-contained page.
xbps keeps no log.
.TP
.B briefing
What the next upgrade will do: kernel and reboot, security fixes, news,
unmerged configuration, disk space, the snapshot tool found.
It reads the pending list as of the last repository refresh and never
refreshes.
.B \-\-offline
contacts nothing.
.TP
.B clean
What can be reclaimed: orphans, the package cache, unused runtimes, with
the command for each.
It removes nothing.
.TP
.B restart
Whether a reboot is due, and the services and programs still using files
an upgrade replaced.
It restarts nothing.
.TP
.B configs
Configuration files an upgrade left unmerged under
.IR /etc ,
with the command to compare each.
It merges nothing.
.TP
.B doctor
What works on this machine, one line per check: ok, warn, fail or n/a.
.TP
.B platform
What kind of machine this is: distribution, package manager, init system,
boot, logs, snapshot tools, and the support tier of the package manager.
.SS Looking things up (read\-only)
.TP
.B list
Installed packages.
.B \-\-explicit
shows only what you asked for.
.TP
.BI info " PKG"
Details of one installed package.
.TP
.BI search " QUERY"
Search the repositories, from the indexes on disk.
.TP
.B check
Pending updates, as of the last refresh.
No read command downloads repository metadata; where none is on disk the
answer says so and names the command that downloads it.
.TP
.BI "inspect owns " PATH
Which package owns a file.
.TP
.BI "inspect files " PKG
The files of a package.
.TP
.B inspect orphans
Dependencies that nothing needs any more.
.TP
.BR tree " [" \fICATEGORY\fR [ .\fISUBCATEGORY\fR ]]
Browse software by category, offline.
.TP
.BI names " NAME"
What a package is called on other distributions.
This command sends the name to repology.org.
.SS Changes (gated)
.TP
.BI "install repo " PKG\fR...
Install from the distribution's repositories.
.TP
.BI "remove repo " PKG\fR...
Remove packages.
.TP
.B remove orphans
Remove the dependencies that nothing needs any more.
.TP
.B upgrade
Full system upgrade.
.TP
.B refresh
Refresh the repository indexes only.
.TP
.BI get " NAME"
Choose a source for NAME and install it; with the default order the
distribution's own package wins.
.B \-\-explain
shows the decision and stops.
When no source has NAME the status is 4, with
.B \-\-explain
too.
.TP
.B undo
Take the last package transaction back, on pacman, apt, dnf and zypper.
It restores package versions, not configuration files or data.
If one old version cannot be found, nothing is changed.
.TP
.B snapshot create
Take a filesystem snapshot through snapper, timeshift or zfs.
.B snapshot list
is read\-only; where listing needs root it says so and exits with status 6.
.SS The program itself
.TP
.BR docs " [" \fITOPIC\fR ]
Built\-in documentation.
.B docs \-\-man
prints this manual page.
.TP
.B config
The effective configuration and the file it was loaded from.
.TP
.BI completions " SHELL"
Print a completion script for bash, zsh, fish, elvish or powershell.
.TP
.B capabilities
What this host supports, per package format and snapshot tool.
.TP
.B i
The interactive interface (the optional
.BR yapacc\-tui ).
.TP
.B mcp
The server for AI assistants, over standard input and output (the
optional
.BR yapacc\-mcp ).
An assistant can read and propose.
It can apply only when a configuration file owned by root sets
.B [mcp] apply = \(dqallow\(dq
and the assistant states that you approved.
.SS Other sources
What these commands install is recorded in the ledger, so that it can be
listed, audited and removed.
Native packages are never recorded there.
.B yapacc \-\-help
lists none of them except
.BR ledger .
.TP
.B flatpak list | search | info
Read flatpak.
.TP
.B install flatpak, remove flatpak, update flatpak
Manage flatpak applications (gated).
.TP
.B install appimage, remove appimage, update appimage
Manage AppImages under your home directory (gated).
.TP
.B install foreign, remove foreign, update foreign
Unpack a deb, rpm or snap made for another distribution into a directory
of its own, without touching the system (gated).
No package script is run.
.TP
.B install tx, tx\-apt, tx\-apt\-repo, tx\-apt\-sources, tx\-rpm, tx\-rpm\-repo
The same for several packages at once, from files, URLs or a repository
(gated).
.TP
.B install container
Create a distrobox container from an image (gated).
.TP
.B foreign info | list
Inspect a package file; list the isolated installs.
.TP
.B external list | audit, remove external, update external
Everything the ledger records, in both ledgers.
.TP
.B ledger status | list | head | verify | audit | conflicts
Read the ledger and check its hash chain.
.TP
.B sources apt | rpm
Read APT or RPM repository definitions.
.TP
.B plugins
List the adapter programs found.
.SS Arch only
On another system each of these says so and exits with status 6.
.TP
.B search \-\-aur | \-\-all
Include the AUR in a search.
.TP
.BI query " NAME"
Repository and AUR metadata for one package.
.TP
.B aur deps | review | pkgbuild
Read an AUR package.
.TP
.BI "install aur " NAME
Clone, show the PKGBUILD for review, build in a sandbox, install (gated
twice: the review, then the install).
.TP
.B install local, install alpm, remove alpm
Install package files you have; install or remove through libalpm, in a
build with the alpm feature (gated).
.TP
.B news
Recent Arch Linux news.
.TP
.B mark explicit | dependency
Change a package's install reason (gated).
.TP
.B download
Download packages into the cache without installing them (gated).
.TP
.B cache clean
Clean the package cache (gated).
.TP
.B inspect leaves | facets | deps | rdepends | file\-search | groups | pacdiff | alpm
Closer looks at the package database.
.TP
.B index build | search
A file index of the repositories for repeated searches.
.TP
.B plan install | remove | upgrade, plan apply
Print a plan;
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
saves it;
.B plan apply
validates the file again and applies it through the gate.
.TP
.B plan flatpak | flatpak\-remove | flatpak\-update | appimage | foreign | tx | tx\-apt | tx\-apt\-repo | tx\-apt\-sources | tx\-rpm | tx\-rpm\-repo | container | cross | safety
Print what the matching command would do; change nothing.
.TP
.B safety apply
A staged change: plan, pre\-check, optional btrfs snapshot, gated change,
optional health check.
.TP
.B package scaffold | validate | srcinfo
Help with writing a PKGBUILD.
.TP
.BI tree " CATEGORY.SUBCATEGORY"
The packages of a subcategory.
.TP
.B monitor check
One report of pending updates, for a timer.
It applies nothing.
.TP
.B monitor daemon\-install
Install a systemd timer that runs
.B monitor check
(gated).
Nothing of the kind is installed unless you run this command.
.SS Build repairs
These serve the builds above, and run on any system.
.TP
.B repair
Classify the failures in a build log.
.TP
.B fixes list | push | pull
The repairs recorded on this machine; push and pull go through a git
remote you configure.
.SS Commands with a JSON form
With \fB\-\-json\fR before the command, these print JSON.
Any other command exits with status 2 and prints nothing.
