.SH THE GATE
Every command that changes the system goes through the same steps.
.IP 1. 4
The plan: what will be installed, removed, upgraded or restored.
.IP 2. 4
The package manager's own simulation of that plan.
If the simulation fails, the change is refused before any question.
Removing a package that is not installed is refused before it, with
status 4.
.IP 3. 4
The exact command that is about to run.
.IP 4. 4
The question,
.BR [y/N] ;
Enter means no.
It follows your locale, and English y and yes are always accepted.
.IP 5. 4
The command, through
.B sudo
or
.B doas
when you are not root.
It is run so that the package manager does not ask a second time: the
answer at the gate is the consent.
.IP 6. 4
The package manager writes its log as usual.
.B yapacc
keeps no second record of native changes.
.PP
With
.B \-\-yes
the plan is shown and applied without a question.
With
.B \-\-dryrun
nothing is applied.
With
.BR \-\-script ,
or when standard input is not a terminal, nobody can answer: the command
is refused unless
.B \-\-yes
or
.B \-\-dryrun
was given.
A refusal exits with status 3 and has changed nothing.
.PP
.B \-\-yes
consents to the plan and to nothing else.
The review of a PKGBUILD needs
.BR \-\-no\-review ,
a non\-free licence needs
.BR \-\-non\-free ,
and when a whole upgrade is undone on zypper, packages with no earlier
record are asked about at the terminal.
.PP
The gate is not a sandbox.
Once you answer yes, the package manager runs with the privileges it
always has.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
ok: the command did what was asked.
.TP
.B 1
failed: the operation ran and failed.
.TP
.B 2
usage: bad arguments, a name that is not a package name,
.B \-\-json
on a command without a JSON form, or a configuration file that does not
parse.
.TP
.B 3
refused: a gate or a guard said no; nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 4
not found: the package, file owner, record, topic or plan does not exist;
a package to remove is not installed;
.B get \-\-explain
chose no source.
.TP
.B 5
integrity: a hash, signature or chain check failed.
.TP
.B 6
unavailable: something this command needs is not available: a tool or
optional component that is not installed, a file this user may not read
(run as root), a feature this build lacks, or a command that does not
exist for this package manager.
.PP
The message is printed to standard error as
.BR "error: ..." .
.SH FILES
Read commands write nothing.
.TP
.I ~/.config/yapacc/config.toml
The configuration.
.B yapacc
needs none and never writes this file.
A file that does not parse is an error (status 2); an unknown key is
ignored with a warning.
.B yapacc docs config
lists every key.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/yapacc/
Old packages while an undo runs (deleted when it ends), the throwaway
home directory of a sandboxed build, the file of
.BR "index build" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/yapacc/ledger/
The user ledger: what
.B yapacc
installed outside the package manager.
As root:
.IR /var/lib/yapacc/ledger/ .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/yapacc/isolated/foreign/
Isolated installs, one directory each, with a manifest and a
.I bin/
directory of attributed program names.
As root:
.IR /var/lib/yapacc/isolated/foreign/ .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/yapacc/recipes/, ~/.local/share/yapacc/fixes/
Generated build recipes and recorded build repairs (Arch).
.TP
.I ~/Applications/yapacc/appimages/
AppImages installed through
.BR "install appimage" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/yapacc/plugins/
Searched for adapter programs; never written.
.PP
The four XDG variables move the first four.
.B yapacc
never edits your shell startup files and never adds a hook to your package
manager.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_CACHE_HOME, XDG_STATE_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the configuration, the caches, the ledger and the data live.
.TP
.B LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANG
The letters of the question of the gate.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width
.B list
cuts descriptions to.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Honoured by the interactive interface.
The command line prints no colour codes.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Opened on a recipe when a build fails at a terminal (Arch).
.TP
.B YAPACC_FOREIGN_ROOT
Where isolated installs go.
.TP
.B YAPACC_LIBEXEC
A directory searched first for
.B yapacc\-tui
and
.BR yapacc\-mcp .
.TP
.B YAPACC_BIN
The
.B yapacc
program the two optional components run.
.TP
.B YAPACC_CONTRIB_DIR
A directory with replacements for the helper scripts of
.BR "safety apply" .
.TP
.B SNAP_TARGETS, SNAP_BASE
What
.B safety apply \-\-snapshot\-tag
snapshots and where it keeps the snapshots.
.SH EXAMPLES
None of these changes the system.
.PP
What works on this machine, and why a package is installed:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc doctor
yapacc why bash
.RE
.fi
.PP
What changed since the machine last worked, and the same as a page to
attach to a report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc timeline \-\-since\-boot
yapacc timeline \-\-html > timeline.html
.RE
.fi
.PP
What the next upgrade will do, without contacting any server, then the
plan of the upgrade itself:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc briefing \-\-offline
yapacc upgrade \-\-dryrun
.RE
.fi
.PP
What taking the last transaction back would do:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc undo \-\-dryrun
.RE
.fi
.PP
The plan of an install and of a clean\-up, and where a package would come
from:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc install repo tree \-\-dryrun
yapacc remove orphans \-\-dryrun
yapacc get ripgrep \-\-explain
.RE
.fi
.PP
In a script: JSON, and a run that can never wait for an answer:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc \-\-json doctor
yapacc \-\-script upgrade \-\-dryrun
.RE
.fi
.PP
This manual page, where it is not installed:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yapacc docs \-\-man | man \-l \-
.RE
.fi
.SH LIMITS
.B undo
takes back one transaction, the last one, and only when every old version
can still be found.
dnf and zypper have no archive to fall back on.
There is no undo on apk, xbps and pkg, and no timeline on xbps.
Old packages from snapshot.debian.org are checked against a recorded hash,
not a signature; Launchpad downloads are not verified.
The output says which applies.
.PP
Snapshots are taken through snapper, timeshift or zfs.
Plain btrfs snapshots are listed only.
With
.B "snapshot = \(dqauto\(dq"
a change is refused when the tool that
.B snapshot_provider
names is not usable; with no snapshot tool at all the plan says that the
change will run without a snapshot.
.B yapacc
never restores a snapshot.
.PP
The sandbox for third\-party builds has no seccomp filter and no resource
limits.
.PP
Portage and Nix are detected only: changes are refused there, with the
reason.
OpenBSD and NetBSD are not supported.
.PP
It ran on virtual machines of Arch, Debian 13, Ubuntu 24.04, Fedora 45,
Rocky Linux 9.9, openSUSE Leap 16.1 and Tumbleweed, Alpine 3.24, Void, and
FreeBSD 14.5 and 15.1: not on real hardware, not on another architecture
than x86_64, and not on distributions derived from these.
